{"requirements":[{"id":"node1_internal_time_source","requirement":"Configure node1 as the internal time source for the cluster.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node1 was configured to permit the cluster subnet, listen on UDP/123, and allow NTP through the firewall. Final observations show healthy synchronized chronyd operation and node2 recorded as an active client. Node1 also retained this state through a demonstrated reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aa9de76934954038","cmd-6b632ba967334bef","cmd-abe68922d75c4b51","cmd-14044722f9f14475","cmd-82403597ad484fb1","cmd-3e1861307fb94857"]},{"id":"node2_uses_node1_exclusively","requirement":"Configure node2 to synchronize its clock against node1 instead of a public time server.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Node2's active configuration contains only node1 (10.100.194.10) as a time source. Chrony selected node1, exchanged valid NTP responses, and reported a normal synchronized clock before and after node2's reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c0217d8645014b49","cmd-abe68922d75c4b51","cmd-cfd5ea7bd3a0477f","cmd-83e42586abd14c33"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"No causally unnecessary state-changing command was identified: configuration, firewall changes, service restarts, reboots, and burst sampling all supported implementation or bounded verification. Both reboot protocols were complete and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities. Hygiene is reduced because numerous executor-created diagnostic files remained under /tmp on both nodes; their creation supported evidence collection, but they were not cleaned. The configuration commands also retained rollback copies named /etc/chrony.conf.pre-internal-time; these had a clear safety role, although their continued retention was not assessed by the lifecycle inventory. No failed services or package damage remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-aa9de76934954038","cmd-c0217d8645014b49","cmd-8b941274e5154ca0","cmd-4ccb7ee048db476d","cmd-abe68922d75c4b51","cmd-14044722f9f14475","cmd-2d14c9ef429a4b4f","cmd-82403597ad484fb1","cmd-cfd5ea7bd3a0477f","cmd-3e1861307fb94857","cmd-83e42586abd14c33","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. Node1 demonstrably served NTP to node2, while node2 used node1 as its sole configured and selected source. The provider-wide restart protocol was successfully completed on both nodes, including healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement and observed interruption, changed boot identities, and recovered configuration, service, and synchronization state.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots inventory /tmp but not /etc, so continued existence of the rollback configuration copies is inferred from their successful creation and the absence of a removal command rather than independently inventoried.","The retained diagnostic files are small evidence artifacts; their contents and exact storage impact were not measured."]}
